<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    package="io.quickauth.sdk_flutter">

    <!--
      No SMS permission, deliberately.

      This declared RECEIVE_SMS, and nothing ever used it: auto-read goes through Google's
      SmsRetriever, which reads exactly one message — the one ending in this app's own 11-char
      hash — and needs no permission at all. That is the entire point of the hash.

      Declaring it was not merely redundant. A plugin's manifest is merged into every app that
      depends on it, so every merchant shipped an SMS permission they did not use, and
      RECEIVE_SMS is in Google Play's restricted set: an app declaring it must justify the use
      in a Play Console declaration or be removed from the store. The SDK was creating a review
      problem for its users in exchange for nothing.

      INTERNET is not declared either — Flutter's own manifest already provides it.
    -->

    <application>
        <!--
          WhatsApp delivers a zero-tap or one-tap code by broadcasting to the app, not by SMS,
          so SmsRetriever never sees it. Declared statically rather than registered at runtime
          because zero-tap's whole promise is that the user does nothing: the code can arrive
          while the app is backgrounded or not running, and a runtime receiver would only exist
          when the app already does.

          android:permission restricts who may deliver this broadcast to holders of WhatsApp's
          own permission. Without it the receiver is exported to every app on the device, and
          any of them could hand our SDK an OTP of their choosing — which the app would then
          treat as a code the user legitimately received.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name="io.quickauth.sdk_flutter.WhatsAppOtpReceiver"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="com.whatsapp.otp.permission.SEND_OTP">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="com.whatsapp.otp.OTP_RETRIEVED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>
    </application>
</manifest>
